\section*{Wikipedia geometry item 14: Nagata–Biran conjecture that if \$X\$ is a smooth algebraic surface and \$L\$ is an ample line bund…}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 039, 2026-10-05): In every dimension at least three, the ordinary multipoint Seshadri constant is claimed to reach $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ at very general points for every sufficiently large $r$. Manuscript 2 (family 039, 2026-09-23): For any polarized smooth complex projective surface, the equal-weight multipoint Seshadri constant is claimed to reach $\sqrt{L^2/r}$ for all integers beyond a polarization-dependent threshold.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Let $X$ be smooth integral projective over $\mathbb C$, with $n=\dim X\ge3$, and $L$ ample. OpenAI claims an integer $r_0(X,L)$ such that for every $r\ge r_0$ and very general ordered distinct $p_1,\ldots,p_r$,
\[
\varepsilon(X,L;\mathbf p)=\left(\frac{L^n}{r}\right)^{1/n}.
\]
The multipoint Seshadri constant is the supremum of $t\ge0$ for which $\pi^*L-t\sum_iE_i$ is nef on the blow-up at these points. For each $r$, the exceptional tuples lie in a union, indexed by a countable set, of proper algebraic closed subsets with nonempty complement. The threshold depends on $(X,L)$ and is not specified uniformly.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 2.} For any smooth integral projective complex surface $S$ and ample line bundle $L$, OpenAI claims $r_0(S,L)$ such that every $r\ge r_0$ and very general tuple of distinct points give, on the blow-up $\pi$ with exceptional curves $E_i$,
\[
\pi^*L-\sqrt{L^2/r}\sum_{i=1}^rE_i\text{ nef},\qquad
\varepsilon(S,L;\mathbf p)=\sqrt{L^2/r}.
\]
The constant is the supremum of $t$ making $\pi^*L-t\sum E_i$ nef. Very general excludes countably many proper closed subsets of the distinct-point configuration space; the statement is for sufficiently many points, not every prescribed small $r$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 039, 2026-10-05): Graded exponent supports are reshaped into a volume-preserving simplex compatible with section multiplication. Full density forces every sufficiently deep interior lattice point to occur, allowing torus interpolation to separate the needed jets; very-general tuples inherit all the finite tests.

Manuscript 2 (family 039, 2026-09-23): Finite jet tests are transferred to an algebraic torus through nodal-curve degeneration. A varying exponent region is tuned to a primitive lattice direction and a subgroup with exactly $r$ points, giving injectivity for all required orders rather than only a fixed multiplicity.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 039, 2026-10-05): The embedded surface companion gives sqrt(L\({}^{2}\)/r) for all sufficiently large r. The catalogue formula uses an unspecified “degree d” as d/sqrt(r); no normalization equating d with sqrt(L\({}^{2}\)) is established, so exact full equivalence is withheld.

Manuscript 2 (family 039, 2026-09-23): The paper proves maximal multipoint constants on every smooth polarized complex surface beyond a surface-dependent threshold. The fragment writes a degree-based constant without a precise definition, so equivalence with the paper's sqrt(L squared/r) formulation is not safely established.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 039, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected plane-curve inequality allows unequal multiplicities and repeated components at every allowed point count. A separate statement gives eventual maximality on each polarized complex surface, with a surface-dependent threshold; the higher-dimensional and positive-characteristic companions are not selected here. Manuscript 1 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 2. Comparator files: MaximalSeshadriConstants.lean, Nagata.lean. Declarations: OAI.MaximalSeshadri.Geometry.maximalSeshadriConstants, OAI.Nagata.nagata\_conjecture.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

